{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-15659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/cf3cc180e5489aa83c17b47db641aae68d03b095085102a29a2942a783369c07.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-15659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The addition rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(3)={f}^{′}(3)+{g}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2,4)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2.5,4)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/19a32fa6a7b2b6481052ab3539838bb00d486f92ea3f08b54efa2a1cfc69d221.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-2}{4-2}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2.5,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-1}{4-2.5}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(3)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & ={f}^{′}(3)+{g}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-15659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "033a08bff804bbe967d931eea365fec1e578d9ea9aa80921f9042a72d6e0087f"} {"question" "q-15659" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-15659" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-15659" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-18603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/c4585c2a9cf0a66da02f96a17dad5986ed8c401a844276f08b367eed765c111d.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)-g(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-18603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)-{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/299b996bf08e4b4e8560d013829ddd1f42751767cd3f00dcd04c23c95f2c62b4.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-1}{3-(-2)}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{3-(-2)}{3-(-2)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)-{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-18603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "033a08bff804bbe967d931eea365fec1e578d9ea9aa80921f9042a72d6e0087f"} {"question" "q-18603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-18603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-18603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-104813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/931802565a13dac284496f41ebb19238402056689a96288b2fbb1dd9f018ae12.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-104813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(-1)=0$ and $g(-1)=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,2)$ equals $\\frac{2}{3}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-104813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "033a08bff804bbe967d931eea365fec1e578d9ea9aa80921f9042a72d6e0087f"} {"question" "q-104813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-104813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-104813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-105502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/7b44dcfdfef91ada119ea7346b59f337a82bc8cca1e364fd6ea29c9eead5a2fd.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-105502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-3)={f}^{′}(-3)\\cdot g(-3)+f(-3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-3)=-2$ and $g(-3)=0.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-3)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,-2)$ equals $3.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-3)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-3) & ={f}^{′}(-3)\\cdot g(-3)+f(-3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-105502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "033a08bff804bbe967d931eea365fec1e578d9ea9aa80921f9042a72d6e0087f"} {"question" "q-105502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-105502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-105502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-344896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/847d1ae3ff7602ae8793ec356dd3034650d8ba85adcb3be579097e46728667a0.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(3)$ where $p(x)=f(x)g(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(3)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-344896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(3)={f}^{′}(3)\\cdot g(3)+f(3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $g(3)=1.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $2.$ So, ${g}^{′}(3)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(3) & ={f}^{′}(3)\\cdot g(3)+f(3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bbadbe4c71739671f1e531f3f554df86ff098c02e95eff09f8b92abbeb4698f"} {"question" "q-344896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "033a08bff804bbe967d931eea365fec1e578d9ea9aa80921f9042a72d6e0087f"} {"question" "q-344896" "field" 